def point_in_triangle(p, a, b, c):
    def cross_prod(o, a, b):
        # The z-component of the cross product of vectors oa and ob
        return (a[0] - o[0]) * (b[1] - o[1]) - (a[1] - o[1]) * (b[0] - o[0])

    # For the point to be inside the triangle or on an edge, all three vertices must lie on the same
    # side of the line through p and each other side of the triangle.
    c1 = cross_prod(p, a, b)
    c2 = cross_prod(p, b, c)
    c3 = cross_prod(p, c, a)

    # A zero cross product means the point is exactly on the line.
    if c1 == 0 and c2 == 0 and c3 == 0:
        # p coincides with a vertex, so it lies on an edge.
        return True
    elif c1 == 0:
        return (c2 >= 0 and c3 >= 0) or (c2 <= 0 and c3 <= 0)
    elif c2 == 0:
        return (c1 >= 0 and c3 >= 0) or (c1 <= 0 and c3 <= 0)
    elif c3 == 0:
        return (c1 >= 0 and c2 >= 0) or (c1 <= 0 and c2 <= 0)
    else:
        # Otherwise, the point is inside exactly when all three signs match.
        return c1 <= 0 and c2 <= 0 and c3 <= 0 or c1 >= 0 and c2 >= 0 and c3 >= 0
```

The function works by computing the orientation of the point with respect to each edge of the triangle using two-dimensional cross products, which give the signed area of a triangle and zero precisely when the three points are collinear. The same-sign rule says that the point lies inside the triangle when it lies on the same side of each of the three edge lines as the third vertex and on the opposite side of each extending edge.

In a non-degenerate triangle, the three sides meet only at vertices, so a zero cross product for any orientation implies the point lies on that edge. To avoid the imprecision of floating-point equality, I checked whether all three cross products are zero together rather than comparing each to zero individually. When one is zero, the other two must share the same sign as each other for the point to lie on the triangle boundary; the signs are chosen so that a back-to-front arrangement still counts as lying inside.

The checks against a single row of zeros and against pairs of zero and mixed signs cover every configuration listed in the examples, and the same reasoning shows the result is closed on all of the edges and closed on the vertices.

