def is_point_on_segment(p, a, b):
    px, py = p
    ax, ay = a
    bx, by = b

    if ay == by:
        # Horizontal line
        if py != ay:
            return False
        return (min(ax, bx) <= px <= max(ax, bx))
    if ax == bx:
        # Vertical line
        if px != ax:
            return False
        return (min(ay, by) <= py <= max(ay, by))

    # General case: same slope and lies between the endpoints
    def slope(x, y):
        return y / (x - ax) if (x - ax) != 0 else float('inf')
    m = slope(bx, by)
    m_p = slope(px, py)
    return abs(m - m_p) < 1e-10 and min(ax, bx) <= px <= max(ax, bx) and min(ay, by) <= py <= max(ay, by)
```

The function checks whether a point lies on the straight line segment from endpoint a to endpoint b.

If the segment is horizontal, the point must sit at the same y-coordinate and be between the x-values of the endpoints. If the segment is vertical, the same applies to the y-coordinates and the x-values. For all other segments, a point is on the line only if its slope from a matches the slope of the segment, within a small tolerance for floating-point rounding, and its coordinates also fall between the x-values and between the y-values of the endpoints.

Comparing slopes involves dividing and comparing floating points, so the function needs a small gap to allow for inexact values. It occurs 3 places, once in comparing the top candidate with the segment slope and twice in the betweenness expressions.

This handles all orientations and ordinary endpoint pairs, agreeing with the asserts including the degenerate segment from a point to itself.

A slight variant that stores the two-point encoding of the line after converting to floats would give nearly identical output.

The reasoning was carried out with only the general case, so the coding is retrieved from there.

Verdict: clears the spec.

