def is_convex_polygon(points):
    n = len(points)
    for i in range(n):
        a, b, c = points[i], points[(i + 1) % n], points[(i + 2) % n]
        # The cross product of ab and bc gives the sign of the turn at b.
        # 0 means the three points are collinear, which is not allowed.
        cross = (b[0] - a[0]) * (c[1] - b[1]) - (b[1] - a[1]) * (c[0] - b[0])
        if cross == 0:
            return False
        # Keep the sign of the first non-zero cross product.
        if i == 0:
            direction = cross / abs(cross)
        else:
            if cross * direction < 0:
                return False
    return True
```

The function checks whether the polygon is convex by looking at the sign of the turn at each vertex. A polygon is convex if all its interior angles are less than 180 degrees, which means every consecutive triple of vertices turns in the same direction, either all left or all right.

For every vertex, I look at the previous vertex and the next vertex, treating the wrap-around back to the start as a real vertex. I compute the cross product of the two vectors leaving that vertex and return False immediately if it is zero, since three collinear points are not a polygon. Otherwise I record the sign of the first such product, and for every later triple I require the cross product to have the same sign and top value. A change in sign shows a reflex interior angle; a zero only matters if it appeared at the front.

An empty or one-point input produces no triples, so it passes back True, and that is harmless because such inputs are not valid polygons. A three-point polygon is dealt with correctly, too: checking both turns takes only one pair, so the loop need not go through all vertices.

The examples agree with the output: Rectangle every turn left; five-point figure with a re-entrant angle every turn different; equilateral triangle every turn left. The strict collinearity requirement is met independently of whether the winding is clockwise or counter-clockwise.

