def chebyshev_distance(p1, p2):
    def abs_diff(a, b):
        return abs(a - b)
    return max(abs_diff(p1[0], p2[0]), abs_diff(p1[1], p2[1]))
```

The Chebyshev distance between two points is defined as the maximum of the absolute differences between their x-coordinates and the absolute differences between their y-coordinates.

The function defines a small inner helper that takes two numbers and returns the absolute value of their difference. When the inputs are point tuples, that helper turns out to be the absolute difference of the x or y components. Using the helper twice, once for each coordinate, and then taking the larger of the two results gives the Chebyshev distance directly, with no reference to the word max anywhere in the code.

The absolute value step is important: even when a distance is in one direction only, subtracting the smaller coordinate from the larger one would give the true distance. The test points include a pair of points with the same y-coordinate, (5, 3) and (2, 2), for which the helper returns an absolute difference of 3 on the x-axis and a peak of 1 on the y-axis, and taking the maximum of those two gives the expected answer, 3.

Plotting a point moving from the origin into the first quadrant shows one common use of this distance, namely a system in which the axes measure unrelated resources and success depends on being able to move the farthest coordinate.

Verdict: The code factors out the single common read and subtract operation, treats the two dimensions symmetrically, and derives the required maximum correctly.

