def parse_ini_line(line):
    # Cut off any trailing newline and strip whitespace from both ends
    trimmed = line.rstrip('\n').strip()
    # Find the = character by skipping over whitespace on the left side
    for i in range(len(trimmed)):
        if trimmed[i] == '=':
            key = trimmed[:i].strip()
            value = trimmed[i+1:].strip()
            # The equal sign is between the key and value, so one of them
            # must have been non-empty before stripping, so there is some text
            return (key, value)
    # No equal sign, so key is all of the trimmed line and value is empty
    return (trimmed, '')
```

The line is first folded up so that leading and trailing whitespace and any newline are gone, and it is then scanned from the left for the equals sign. Because the key is guaranteed not to contain whitespace, the first equals sign is the one that divides key from value. The part of the line before it becomes the key and the part after becomes the value, each then stripped to remove any accidental whitespace that the specification allows on either side. If no equals sign is found, the whole line is read as the key and the value is an empty string.

