def undo_redo_state(commands):
    document = []
    redo = []
    cleared = True
    for name, *args in commands:
        if name == "do":
            value = args[0]
            document.append(value)
            redo.clear()  # "clears any pending redo history"
        elif name == "undo":
            if document:
                redo.append(document.pop())  # Move last value to redo
        elif name == "redo":
            if redo:
                document.append(redo.pop())  # Move top of redo back
    return document
```

Each command updates a document list and an undo buffer, which stores the values that have been taken off the document by undo commands.

A "do" command adds a value to the end of the document and empties whatever values were waiting in the undo buffer. An "undo" command moves the last value from the document into the undo buffer, if the document is not already empty. A "redo" command moves the value most recently sent to the undo buffer back onto the end of the document (popping from one end and appending to the other). Because it is a last-value listening history rather than a full state stack, repeated undo or redo has a natural stopping point: once the undo buffer is empty, redo does nothing, and once the document is empty, undo also does nothing.

